    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, state, attempt, next_attempt_at,\n          lease_until, provider_msg_id, sent_at, idem_key UNIQUE, updated_at)\n```\n\n- `start_at_utc` is stored in UTC; `tz` is the IANA zone of the appointment (needed for \"9am local\" rules and for display).\n- `idem_key` is deterministic: `sha256(appointment_id || channel || fire_at_utc || version)`. This is the anchor for dedup.\n- `state \u2208 {pending, leased, sent, failed, cancelled}`.\n\n## Scheduling and time zones\n\n- All scheduling math is done in UTC. `fire_at_utc = start_at_utc - lead_time`.\n- The user's `tz` is used only for (a) rendering the local time in the message body and (b) computing \"9am local\" style rules, which are converted to UTC at write time.\n- DST is handled by the tz database: a \"9am local\" reminder on a spring-forward day still fires at 9am local; the UTC instant shifts. We never store local times as the source of truth.\n- If the appointment is rescheduled, we bump `version`, which changes `idem_key` for future reminders and cancels any pending ones for the old version.\n\n",
